\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). Within the fully v2 BN cells, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach OCC 0.767; Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.108, 0.014]$, crossing zero and thus not individually significant at $\alpha{=}0.05$ given the small sample). The LN cells show the same directional pattern ($-$2.5\,pp), yielding a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type, with the BN cell showing a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though Full OCC+LN has $n{=}1$ (v2) and Detach+LN has $n{=}3$ (v1, marked $^\star$). We report effect sizes and confidence intervals for transparency; the small-$n$ factorial design prioritizes breadth across a 12-condition space over statistical power for individual pairwise tests. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern.

\textbf{Ordinal, not additive.} Both BN and LN lines slope downward from Gradient OFF to Gradient ON without crossing (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}), confirming an ordinal pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though Full OCC+LN has only $n{=}1$ (v2) and Detach+LN has $n{=}3$ (v1, marked $^\star$); whether normalization type modulates the gradient flow effect requires additional seeds to confirm.

